# Rate limiting

Runtime: fastapi-limiter on the released 0.1.x API (`uv add fastapi-limiter`):
`await FastAPILimiter.init(redis)` once per process, then
`Depends(RateLimiter(times=, seconds=))` on routers or endpoints. Endpoints need no extra
`Request` parameter. The 0.0.x decorator API (`@limiter.limit`) and the unreleased
pyrate-limiter rewrite do not apply.

Install with `python-redis`: the Redis client stays in
`project/infrastructure/adapters/acache.py` (`redis_client()`) — one client, one `REDIS_*`
Settings contract — and the limiter lifecycle lives in
`project/infrastructure/adapters/rate_limit.py`. Counters share the app key prefix.

```python
from fastapi_limiter import FastAPILimiter

from project.infrastructure.adapters.acache import redis_client
from project.settings import Constants


async def init_rate_limiter() -> None:
    await FastAPILimiter.init(redis_client(), prefix=f"{Constants.REDIS_KEY_PREFIX}:rl")


async def close_rate_limiter() -> None:
    await FastAPILimiter.close()
```

Expose both as `LazyService` entries in `project/container.py`
(`python-architecture/python-di.mdc`) and await them in the app lifespan in
`project/infrastructure/apps/api.py`. Presentation never imports `redis` or the adapter
module.

```python
@asynccontextmanager
async def lifespan(app: FastAPI) -> AsyncIterator[None]:
    setup_logging()
    await Container().init_rate_limiter()
    yield
    await Container().close_rate_limiter()
```

## Applying limits

```python
router = APIRouter(dependencies=[Depends(RateLimiter(times=10, seconds=60))])


@router.post("/orders/v1", dependencies=[Depends(RateLimiter(times=2, seconds=1))])
async def create_order(...) -> ...:
```

- Several `RateLimiter`s on one route evaluate in order — strictest (smallest window and
  count) first.
- The default identifier is client IP + path (honors `X-Forwarded-For`); override
  `identifier=` in `FastAPILimiter.init` to key by API token or user id on authenticated
  APIs.
- Keep the default callback (raises `HTTPException` 429 with `Retry-After`) so the app
  exception handlers shape the body (`python-fastapi`).
- WebSockets: `WebSocketRateLimiter` as the dependency, `WebSocketRateLimitException` on
  breach.
- When limits differ per environment, read them from `Settings` and build the dependency
  when constructing the router instead of repeating literals.

## layers.toml

```toml
[libs.fastapi_limiter]
allowed_in = ["presentation", "adapters"]
```

`redis` stays adapters/repositories only (`python-redis`).

## Tests

Assert limits in e2e (`python-tests`) with the Redis testcontainer fixture
(`python-redis`): N allowed calls, then 429 with `Retry-After`; `flushdb` between tests. Do
not disable the limiter or `patch` `RateLimiter`.
